*.h linguist-language=cpp

# Shell scripts are checked out with LF endings on every platform. A CRLF here is
# not cosmetic: the carriage return becomes part of the interpreter path, so
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash` fails as "bad interpreter" and the script will not run at
# all on Linux. install.sh is the supported way to build and install from source,
# so it has to survive being committed from any machine, including this one.
*.sh text eol=lf

# ── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalize every text file to LF IN THE REPOSITORY, regardless of what any
# individual clone has core.autocrlf set to. Checkout is unaffected: with
# autocrlf=true this machine still gets CRLF working files and editors see no
# change. What this pins is what gets COMMITTED.
#
# This is a rule rather than a one-time pass because a one-time pass drifts -
# it holds only until someone clones with a different setting, and the drift is
# invisible in diffs until the day it is a whole-file rewrite nobody can review.
#
# Surveyed before writing this: 470 of 474 tracked text files were ALREADY LF in
# the index, so the tree was never mixed - core.autocrlf was doing its job. The
# two exceptions are normalized in the same commit. Source was never affected:
# every .h and .cpp checked read `i/lf w/crlf`, which is autocrlf working, not
# drift.
* text=auto

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text. Git already auto-detects these
# two; naming them means a future addition of the same kind cannot be
# misdetected by an empty-file or short-header edge case.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
